Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Your upper body is moving forward but legs dipped slightly — keep pushing.",
  "summary": "Solid week of training overall with 28 sets logged and over 10,700 kg of total tonnage moved. At 28 years old with a healthy BMI of 23.5, you are in a great position to build strength efficiently, and this period reflects that potential. Chest and shoulders made small but meaningful gains in strength index, while back, biceps, and triceps held steady. The one area to monitor is legs, which saw a minor dip in strength index from 108 to 107. Volume distribution is fairly well-balanced across the major muscle groups, which is a good foundation for your long-term strength goal. Keep the momentum going and address the small imbalances highlighted below.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "status": "on_track",
    "notes": "Chest improved from 104 to 105 and shoulders ticked up from 101 to 102, showing continued forward progress on upper body pushing movements. Back, biceps, and triceps all held their previous index values, indicating maintenance rather than regression, which is acceptable for a single period. The only concern is legs, which slipped slightly from 108 to 107 — this is a minor dip and not yet cause for alarm, but it is worth watching closely next period to ensure it does not become a trend. Overall, progressive overload is on track with no significant regressions."
  },
